<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Milan</h1><p class="st-meta">1259 CE – 1395 CE · capital Milan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The signoria through which the Visconti turned Milan into the strongest state in northern Italy, before buying a ducal title in 1395.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1259 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martino-della-torre">Martino della Torre</a> was made lord of the people, turning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-milan">Commune of Milan</a> into a lordship. The della Torre were challenged by Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottone-visconti">Ottone Visconti</a>, who defeated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-desio">Desio</a> in 1277; his great-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-i-visconti">Matteo I Visconti</a> received the title of imperial vicar in 1294.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Visconti ruled through councils and appointed podestà while keeping communal forms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti">Azzone Visconti</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-bergamo">Bergamo</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-cremona">Cremona</a>, Como, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-brescia">Brescia</a> and other cities in the 1330s. Archbishop Giovanni Visconti added <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-bologna">Bologna</a>, and in 1359 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ii-visconti">Galeazzo II</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> overthrew his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò</a> in 1385 and created a centralised state, beginning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> in 1386.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1395 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo</a> bought the title of duke from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia">Emperor Wenceslas</a>, turning the lordship into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan</a>, which he expanded towards <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> before his death in 1402.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-milan.jpg" alt="Lordship of Milan" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Lordship_of_Milan_(XIII_century-1395).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1259 CE – 1395 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Milan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Matteo I Visconti, Azzone Visconti, Gian Galeazzo Visconti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lombard, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy, Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Signoria of Milan, Visconti Milan</dd></dl></aside></div>
